erms:\n\n- t^4: 64t^4\n\n- t^3: 32t^3\n\n- t^2: 4t^2 -64t^2 = -60t^2\n\n- t: -16t\n\n- Constants: 16\n\nTherefore, the expanded RHS is:\n\n64t^4 + 32t^3 -60t^2 -16t +16\n\nSo, going back:\n\nOriginal equation after squaring both sides:\n\n3(1 - t^2) = 64t^4 + 32t^3 -60t^2 -16t +16\n\nLeft-hand side (LHS) before expansion was:\n\n3 - 3t^2\n\nThus, equation:\n\n3 - 3t^2 = 64t^4 + 32t^3 -60t^2 -16t +16\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n0 = 64t^4 +32t^3 -60t^2 -16t +16 -3 +3t^2\n\nSimplify:\n\n64t^4 +32t^3 + (-60t^2 +3t^2) + (-16t) + (16 -3)\n\n= 64t^4 +32t^3 -57t^2 -16t +13\n\nThus, the equation is:\n\n64t^4 +32t^3 -57t^2 -16t +13 =0\n\nThis is a quartic equation in t. Quartic equations can be challenging to solve analytically. Perhaps factorable, or rational roots possible.\n\nPossible rational roots can be found using Rational Root Theorem: possible roots are ±1, ±1/2, ±1/4, ±13, etc. Test these.\n\nLet me plug t =1:\n\n64 +32*(-1) -57 -16 +13 = 64 -32 -57 -16 +13 = (64 +13) - (32 +57 +16) = 77 - 105 = -28 ≠0\n\nt=1/2:\n\n64*(1/16) +32*(1/8) -57*(1/4) -16*(1/2)+13 = 4 +4 -14.25 -8 +13 = (8) - (14.25 +8) +13= 8 -22.25 +13= -1.25 ≠0\n\nt=1/4:\n\n64*(1/256) +32*(1/64) -57*(1/16) -16*(1/4)+13 ≈≈ Approximately 0.25 +0.5 -3.5625 -4 +13 ≈ 5.75 -7.5625≈-1.8125 ≠0\n\nt= -1:\n\n64 +32*(-1) -57*(-1)^2 -16*(-1) +13=64 -32 -57 +16 +13= (64 +16 +13) - (32 +57)=93 -89=4≠0\n\nt= -1/2:\n\n64*(1/16) +32*(-1/8) -57*(1/4) -16*(-1/2)+13=4 -4 -14.25 +8 +13= (0 +0 -14.25) +21=6.75≠0\n\nt= -1/4:\n\n64*(1/256) +32*(-1/4) etc too tedious, probably not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps factor by grouping:\n\n64t^4 +32t^3 -57t^2 -16t +13\n\nGroup terms: (64t^4 +32t^3) + (-57t^2 -16t) +13\n\nFactor from first group: 32t^3(2t +1)\n\nWait, 64t^4 = 32t^3 * 2t, 32t^3*1? Wait, 32t^3*(2t +1) = 64t^4 +32t^3. Then the remaining terms: -57t^2 -16t +13. Not obvious. Maybe factor out -1 from the third term:\n\nHmm, 32t^3(2t +1) -t(57t +16) +13. Not helpful. Alternatively, try synthetic division with potential ro